endue.formats pack readers (lib/packs)

These readers were written for endue Desk from published descriptions of the formats. No code was
taken or followed from squashfs-tools, squashfs-tools-ng, wimlib, liblzo2, 7-Zip, libmspack or any
other source under the GPL or the LGPL, and no permissively licensed implementation was followed.

Descriptions followed

* SquashFS 4.0: the on-disk format as its authors document it (Phillip Lougher's
  Documentation/filesystems/squashfs.rst in the Linux kernel and the squashfs-tools README): the
  superblock, metadata blocks, inode and directory tables, fragment, id and export tables, and the
  compression ids and their options.

* LZO1X: the stream format as documented in Documentation/staging/lzo.rst of the Linux kernel
  (instruction encoding, literal and match forms, the end marker).

* AppImage: the AppImage specification (https://github.com/AppImage/AppImageSpec): the type 1 and
  type 2 magic, the runtime's .upd_info and .sha256_sig sections; ELF headers from the System V ABI.

* XPRESS Huffman: [MS-XCA] Xpress Compression Algorithm, Microsoft Open Specifications, section 2.2
  (LZ77+Huffman decompression) and its section 3.2 examples.

* LZX: [MS-PATCH] LZX DELTA Compression and Decompression, Microsoft Open Specifications, sections
  2.1 to 2.7 (blocks, trees and pretrees, position slots, aligned offsets, E8 translation), with
  the WIM variant's differences (each chunk independent, E8 translation always on with size
  12,000,000, the 1-bit default block size).

* WIM: Microsoft's Windows Imaging File Format (WIM) description: the header, resource headers, the
  lookup table, the XML data, the metadata resource's security data and directory entries.

The test fixtures are data made with the tools in a container or through liblzo2
(tests/packs/make-fixtures.sh, tests/packs/make-lzo.py,
plugins/archives/tests/make-fixtures-linux.sh); the tools are not part of this repository.
